{
  "trace_id": "hybrid::q15",
  "architecture": "hybrid",
  "question": "The KV cache is described as making per-token generation roughly linear rather than quadratic in cost \u2014 given that, why can't a serving system just treat continuous batching as an independent scheduling optimization with no coupling to how the cache is managed?",
  "answer": "Continuous\u2011batching is not just a \u201cplug\u2011in\u201d scheduler; it must constantly add new requests to a running batch and drop finished ones.\u202fEach request carries its own KV\u2011cache state (the keys and values for all tokens it has already generated), and that state has to be allocated when the request starts, kept for the whole time it is in the batch, and freed as soon as the request finishes.\u202fIf the batching system ignored this, it would either have to recompute the cache (undoing the linear\u2011cost benefit) or would run out of memory because the cache for many concurrent requests would pile up unchecked.\u202fThus, continuous batching \u201cdepends heavily on efficient per\u2011request KV\u2011cache management\u201d\u202f\u2014\u202fthe scheduler must track and free each request\u2019s cache independently, and any reshuffling or recomputation of cache state would erase the latency gains that continuous batching is meant to provide\u202f[continuous-batching-and-throughput::1].\n\nIn addition, the KV cache is a major source of memory pressure: \u201cdoubling how many retrieved chunks get included in a prompt \u2026 doubles the KV cache memory that request consumes \u2026 directly limiting how many concurrent requests a fixed amount of GPU memory can serve\u201d\u202f[kv-cache-and-why-it-matters-for-serving::1]. Because continuous batching changes the set of active requests on the fly, the serving system must manage this memory carefully; treating batching as independent of cache management would lead to memory exhaustion or forced cache eviction, again destroying the linear\u2011cost advantage.\n\nTherefore, a serving system cannot treat continuous batching as a completely independent optimization; it must tightly couple the batching logic with KV\u2011cache allocation, tracking, and freeing to preserve both the latency benefits of dynamic batching and the memory efficiency of the KV cache.",
